window.__finlexDocLoaded(10221,{"id":10221,"articles":[{"id":1000915,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Establishes the two-tier US GAAP consolidation framework: first evaluate the Variable Interest Entity (VIE) model (primary beneficiary = power + economics), then the Voting Interest Entity (VOE) model (>50% voting shares).","seq":0},{"id":1000916,"no":"ASC 810-10","heading":"Dual Consolidation Model: VIE Primary Beneficiary & VOE","text":"• Step 1 — VIE Model (ASC 810-10-15 / 25): An entity is a VIE if equity at risk is insufficient, equity holders lack decision-making power/obligation to absorb losses/right to receive residual returns, or voting rights are disproportionate. The Primary Beneficiary consolidates when it has BOTH (a) power to direct activities that most significantly impact economic performance AND (b) obligation to absorb losses or right to receive benefits that could potentially be significant.\n• Step 2 — Voting Interest Model: If not a VIE, consolidation generally requires >50% of voting shares (no 'de facto' control concept).\n• Deconsolidation (ASC 810-10-40): Full gain/loss in P&L with retained investment remeasured to fair value.","seq":1},{"id":1000917,"no":"US GAAP vs. IFRS / CAS","heading":"Key Cross-Framework Differences","text":"• Major Divergence from IFRS 10 / CAS 33: IFRS 10 uses a SINGLE control model (including 'de facto' control with <50% voting rights and consideration of substantive potential voting rights); ASC 810 uses a DUAL VIE/VOE model and generally ignores de facto control and potential voting rights under the VOE model.","seq":2},{"id":1000918,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":3}]});
